{
  "working": "Identify geometry and hybridisation: tetrahedral complexes (e.g., [CoCl4]2-, [NiCl4]2-) adopt sp3 hybridisation; square planar (e.g., [PtCl4]2-) adopt dsp2; octahedral (e.g., [Cr(H2O)6]3+, [V(H2O)6]3+, [Fe(H2O)6]3+) adopt d2sp3; complexes with strong field ligands like CN- give low-spin octahedral (e.g., [Fe(CN)6]4-) not sp3; Ni(CO)4 is tetrahedral but Ni(0) d10 diamagnetic. Paramagnetism depends on unpaired electrons: For tetrahedral d-electron configurations, high-spin is typical; Co2+ (d7) in tetrahedral has 3 unpaired electrons → paramagnetic; Ni2+ (d8) in tetrahedral has 2 unpaired electrons → paramagnetic. Square planar d8 (Pt2+) is diamagnetic; octahedral low-spin d6 (Fe2+ in CN-) is diamagnetic; octahedral high-spin d3 (Cr3+) and d4 (V3+) are paramagnetic but geometry is octahedral, not sp3. Thus only tetrahedral complexes with paramagnetism and sp3 hybridisation are considered. Among the list, [CoCl4]2- and [NiCl4]2- are tetrahedral and paramagnetic. [PtCl4]2- is square planar (dsp2) and diamagnetic; [Cr(H2O)6]3+, [V(H2O)6]3+, [Fe(H2O)6]3+ are octahedral; [Fe(CN)6]4- is octahedral low-spin diamagnetic; [Ni(CO)4] is tetrahedral but Ni0 d10 diamagnetic. Hence total number of species that are paramagnetic and have sp3 hybridisation = 2.",
  "answer": "[TYPE: chemistry] [CALC_VALUE: 2] [CALC_UNIT: none] [SUBSTANCE: null]",
  "objection": null
}

